\subtree{
  \title{Introduction forms for immutable arrays}

  \p{After mentioning it in last week's weeknotes, I [got some great feedback](https://mathstodon.xyz/@monoidmusician@tech.lgbt/116309751306965503) from [Verity](https://mathstodon.xyz/@monoidmusician@tech.lgbt) on an introduction form for immutable arrays.  (Again, the biggest constraint here is that we don't want to use lists: Swarm \em{does} have lists, but not built-in in a primitive way.  Using lists requires you to first unlock algebraic data types as well as recursive types, and you should be able to unlock and use arrays independently.)}

\p{After a bit of back and forth, the current best idea is
\pre{\startverb
(Int -> a) -> b -> (b -> Unit + Int * (a -> (a * b))) -> Array a
\stopverb}
  \ul{
    \li{The first argument specifies the starting value at any index.}
    \li{The second argument is a seed value for the unfold.}
    \li{I like to think of the third argument as a protocol for communication between the array constructor and the user, specifying the process for a sort of imperative unfold.
      \ul{
        \li{The array constructor begins by announcing the current seed value of type \code{b}.}
        \li{The user gets to choose whether to stop, or to continue by specifying an index.}
        \li{The array constructor provides the value of type \code{a} currently at that index.}
        \li{The user then gets to provide a new value of type \code{a}, and a new seed.}
      }
    }
  }
  This is trickier to implement than what I had before (\em{e.g.} it requires several new types of CESK machine continuation frames instead of just one, and in practice, we want to wait until we see which indices are requested before deciding on the final size of the array and filling in any missing values), but ultimately should be fine.  I need to finish up the implementation and play around with it: implement some other simpler introduction forms in terms of it, and try using it for things that were inefficient before.
  }
}

\subtree{
  \title{Draft blog post on zero/empty set/void type/monoids etc.}

  \p{Inspired by [a conversation on Mastodon](https://mathstodon.xyz/@cxandru@types.pl/116261508892427847), I've [started drafting a blog post](00CI) on the connections between things like #{\forall} with an empty domain, functions out of empty types, sum/product/and/or of an empty list, \em{etc.}.  None of this will be at all surprising to experienced Haskell programmers or type theorists, but I hope it may be interesting for some who are just learning.}
   \p{As these things always happen, when I started expanding my outline into prose, I found that the very first bullet point in the outline expanded into enough material for an entire blog post.  So it may just be very long, or I may split it up into multiple posts... we'll see!}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Teaching induction}
  \p{I had a lot of fun this week teaching my [Discrete Math](0020) students about [induction principles for natural numbers](00CA).}
  \p{On Wednesday I went over a strong induction principle.  When I teach them, I usually state it like
   ##{P(0) \land (\forall k. (\forall j \leq k. P(j)) \to P(k+1)) \to \forall n. P(n)}
  I find a formulation without an explicit base case more elegant, but I don't think I am brave enough to teach it this way:
  ##{(\forall k. (\forall j < k. P(j)) \to P(k)) \to \forall n. P(n)}
  I think they would be completely confused by the apparent lack of a base case.  Of course, the base case is still there, in the sense that you have to at the very least prove #{P(0)} with no assumptions, since #{\forall j < 0. P(j)} is trivial.  But you would essentially have to almost always start by doing a case split on whether #{n} is 0 anyway, so I feel like I might as well present it in a form with #{P(0)} already split out separately.  But I would be interested to hear about how others teach this.  Of course, in some sense it doesn't matter since the two principles are logically equivalent, but I think it matters in a pedagogical sense.}
  \p{As the [crowning example](00CX), I proved that every natural number can be written as a sum of distinct powers of two... and then showed them that our induction proof is actually a recursive program for calculating the binary expansion of any natural number, by coding it in Disco:
\pre{\startverb
binary : N -> Set(N)
binary(0) = {}
binary(2k) = {e+1 | e in binary(k)}
binary(2k+1) = {0} union {e+1 | e in binary(k)}
\stopverb}
I definitely enjoyed seeing several of them get head-exploding expressions on their faces when they realized the correspondence between invoking the induction hypothesis and making a recursive function call.
}
}

\subtree{
  \title{A polynomial testing principle}
  \p{I've had Conor McBride's paper [A polynomial testing principle](https://personal.cis.strath.ac.uk/conor.mcbride/PolyTest.pdf) on my reading list for a while, and this week I finally got around to reading through it carefully, typing up all the Agda code myself and trying to fill in as much of it as I could before peeking.  I now have some much better ideas about how to encode things nicely in Agda. The topic of the paper was also really fun: I was definitely familiar with the idea that two degree-#{n} polynomials must be equal if they agree at #{n+1} points, but I would not have guessed that this can be proved in a few hundred lines of Agda.  I also enjoyed how it hinged on the forward difference and summation operators, old friends of mine from reading Concrete Mathematics when I was in undergrad.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Piano corner}
  \p{For the past few weeks I have been working on Chopin's 1st piano concerto. This is not the first time I've worked on it, but I haven't in a while.  I have long dreamed of performing it someday.  I always figured this was essentially a pipe dream, but after recently seeing [a video of Olga Scheps playing it with a small chamber orchestra](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2bFo65szAP0), I have a bit more hope that it might be possible! I didn't even realize there was an arrangement for chamber orchestra.  Of course there are arrangements for two pianos as well, though that idea doesn't excite me as much.}
}
